<header class="ref-head"><span class="hero-ornament"></span><h1 class="research-title">27 October in history</h1><p class="ref-lede">5 events that happened on 27 October, from 1275 to 1904.</p></header>
<ol class="day-events"><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1275</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Amsterdam is first mentioned in a charter</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Count Floris V of Holland granted toll exemption to the people living near the dam on the Amstel. The charter is the earliest document to mention Amsterdam, then a small fishing settlement.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1553</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Michael Servetus is burned for heresy in Geneva</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Geneva's council, with John Calvin's backing, had the Spanish physician and theologian Michael Servetus burned for denying the Trinity. His death became a landmark in arguments for religious toleration.</p></li><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1682</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">William Penn arrives to found Pennsylvania</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">William Penn reached the Delaware River aboard the Welcome to take possession of his new colony. He laid out Philadelphia as a planned 'greene Countrie Towne' that later hosted the Declaration of Independence.</p></li><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1787</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">The first Federalist essay is published</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Writing as 'Publius', Alexander Hamilton published the first of 85 essays urging New Yorkers to ratify the new US Constitution. With Madison and Jay he produced the most influential commentary on the Constitution.</p></li><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1904</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">The New York City Subway opens</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">The Interborough Rapid Transit Company opened New York's first underground line, from City Hall to 145th Street. Around 150,000 people rode on the first day of a network that grew into one of the world's largest.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a></div></li></ol>
